 181: ## PART B -- #530 lcaisalane, closing pass 4, repair 4, 111 rows
 182: 
 183: ### R3 -- the forward-count class escaped a THIRD time; the class closure owed at repair 4 was not delivered.
 184: 
 185: Verified on the source: the write arm returns the forward (`dev_lca.c:299-303`); the read arm forwards once and fills
 186: (`:305-311`).  R2w (`if (len != 1)` + a second forward) is the contract-named RD2 gated on the one width its row uses
 187: (`iir lca rd`, LDBU, `:826`).  WT6/WTsplit gate the write fallthrough to the split-tail widths, which the header calls
 188: part of THE CELL SPACE "rowed WHOLE" (`:73-81`) and the scoped x_wfallthru entry (`:101-109`) does not mention.  RD2L0
 189: duplicates the read at lane 0.  All 111/111, each moving exactly its own discriminator channel (`DISC_SUMMARY.out`).
 190: 
 191: **Grade, precisely.**  None of these is a clause-(d) floor breach by the letter: the fix folded `ofs` and added the
 192: `len == 0` refusal; RD2/WT insert routing, weakening nothing the fix depends on.  They are (1) L4 -- the RD2 sentence is
 193: unscoped and POSTTABLE 5's "RD2 at lane 2" overclaims (both already carry 09-15 correction notes, verified), and the
 194: tail cells are inside a space the header claims rowed whole with no disposition; and (2) the repair-chain amendment's
 195: obligation: two consecutive passes of one class owe the class closure, and this is the third.  In-round either way.
 196: The coordinator's "FLOOR" label is corrected so that no future pass cites this chain as precedent for enumerating
 197: routing edits as (d) floor everywhere.
 198: 
 199: ### RB1 -- the 0x61-0x6f stub as a floor oracle: acceptable for LANE 3 ONLY, with the liveness row, recorded as a model oracle.
 200: 
 201: The stub is real and I read it: `dev_pckbc.c:941-951`, PC-style (`bus_isa.c:308-310` passes `pc_style_flag = 1` unless
 202: `BUS_ISA_PCKBC_NONPCSTYLE`), every access to `relative_addr` not in {0, 4} bumps a function-static `x` and bit 0x10 of
 203: the answer is its parity, reads and writes alike.  As a channel it varies under every fallthrough/duplicate mutant and
 204: is stable on base -- the vacuity floor is satisfied.  What it is NOT is durable: it is a `TODO`, shared by every pckbc
 205: instance through one static, and its implementation would vacate every row resting on it in one edit.  The liveness row
 206: turns that from silent to LOUD, which is the harness's own standard, so the stub is admissible.  But:
 207: 
 208: * **Lanes 0-2 use the architectural channels the seat MEASURED** -- lane 0 writes: `kbcq` (exists); lane 0 reads: the
 209:   8042 with TWO replies queued (D, measured at (1,0), (8,0) and tail 6); lane 2 writes: `iir`/`iirw` (exist); lane 2
 210:   reads at len 2/4/8: IIR with TXRDY armed (B, measured) in a NEW session -- do not extend `iirr`, or table 5's RD2 kill
 211:   moves under the targeting rule; lane 1 R and W: CMOS REG C (G) or the 8253 at 0x41 (F), one arming store each.
 212: 
 213:   *** THIS BULLET IS **OVERTURNED** BY THE 2026-09-16 ADJUDICATION (FABLE_C5_RULING_20260915.md, S-B.4): CMOS REG C and
 214:   ns16550 IIR ARE ONE-SHOT LATCHES, AND A LATCH CANNOT CARRY A FORWARD-COUNT CLASS.  `dev_mc146818.c:565` -- the read
 215:   does `d->reg[MC_REGC*4] = 0x00;`, so a second read finds it already zero, and the write path is a plain assignment.
 216:   `dev_ns16550.c:211-213` -- the read clears IIR_TXRDY once; the write path is `d->fcr = idata;`.  **A latch separates
 217:   ZERO accesses from AT LEAST ONE; it can never separate ONE from TWO, which is exactly and only what this class asks.**
 218:   Measured consequence: WWall 169/185 is sixteen stub rows and NOTHING else, RWall 167/185 is 16 stub + 2 tailr, and
 219:   RD2post 161/185 has no architectural carrier at lanes 1-2 -- so the never-sole-carrier requirement in the very next
 220:   bullet was breached at three cells BY THE CHANNELS THIS BULLET NAMED.  Repair 6 moves lanes 0-2 and the tails to the
 221:   8253 per-counter rd_msb/wr_msb ALTERNATORS at 0x40/0x41/0x42, and keeps stub3 + its liveness row for lane 3 ONLY,
 222:   where the 8253 is structurally blind (port 0x43 advances no flip-flop; cdxZL3 150/150, measured).
 223: 
 224:   *** AND THE CORRECTION ITSELF NEEDS CORRECTING, WHICH IS WHY IT IS WRITTEN OUT HERE RATHER THAN SUMMARISED.  The C5
 225:   ruling's L4 item 2 quotes this bullet as naming CMOS and IIR, and it does -- but it names them **as one of two
 226:   options**, "CMOS REG C (G) or the 8253 at 0x41 (F)".  The 8253 was on the table from the start and repair 5 took the
 227:   other branch.  So the defect is NOT that the ruling named an unusable channel; it is that **it offered two channels
 228:   as interchangeable when one of them is structurally incapable of the measurement, and gave no discriminator for
 229:   choosing between them.**  The discriminator now exists and is checkable by reading, which is the part worth carrying
 230:   forward: A FORWARD-COUNT ROW NEEDS A COUNTER OR AN ALTERNATOR, NEVER A LATCH.  The never-sole-carrier PRINCIPLE
 231:   stands untouched; only the channel list under it was wrong. ***
 232: * **Lane 3 direct, both arms, all four widths: the stub at 0x63/0x67/0x6b/0x6f**, plus the MANDATORY liveness row (two
 233:   plain flat reads flip 0x10), plus a contract sentence: "an emulator-model oracle (a TODO counter), not a hardware
 234:   contract; the liveness row reds when port 0x61 is implemented and these rows are then re-derived".  That is the
 235:   `0xffffffff` precedent from the carrier -- an emulator-compatibility oracle, named as such.
 236: * Stub rows at lanes 0-2 are permitted as ADDITIONAL strengthening (they close the data axis cheaply) but may not be the
 237:   SOLE carrier of any lane 0-2 cell.  Cost: about three sessions more than the all-stub plan.  Benefit: the blast radius
 238:   of the TODO being implemented is eight rows plus a liveness row, and 32 floor rows do not rest on one static.
 239: 
 240: ### RB2 -- the enumeration is COMPLETE for the forward-count class on the stated scope; four scope sentences are owed.
 241: 
 242: The (arm x width-kind x lane) table in the brief is the right one and I re-derived its shape: heads are always lane 3
 243: and tails always lane 0 because `memory_rw` splits at 1 KB and `(0x400k - h) >> 5 = 0x20k - 1`; direct widths are the
 244: CPU's {1, 2, 4, 8}; `len == 0` has no producer.  Cells: direct 4 lanes x 4 widths x 2 arms = 32; tails 3/5/6/7 x 2 arms
 245: = 8; heads EQUIVALENT (the only mapped 0x1f-mod-0x20 ports are 0x3ff/0x2ff = ns16550 SCR, whose `default:` arm at
 246: `dev_ns16550.c:298-302` reads `d->reg[relative_addr]` with no side effect -- verified; 0x60-0x6f holds no such port).
 247: The contract writes down: (1) the machine as probed -- alphabook1, no IDE image (so no wdc: `bus_isa.c:275-286`), no
 248: X11 (no VGA); (2) the width set and the split derivation; (3) `len == 0` disclosed; (4) the DIRECTION swap (a read
 249: routed through the write arm) is a different class and is caught by the existing value rows -- one sentence, predicted
 250: (every 4-byte read row answers 0), not a row.  Two things the seat did not check, settled here: the lpt read arm
 251: (`dev_lpt.c:79-88`) returns `d->data` or constants -- no side effect, so it is neither a channel nor a gap; IDE-attached
 252: behaviour is OUT OF SCOPE and disclosed (wdc 0x1f7 lane 3, altstatus 0x3f6 lane 2 would add channels, not cells).
 253: 
 254: ### RB3 -- repair 5 owes kill table 6 AND closing pass 5.  The "derived from the handler" argument is what pass 5 checks.
