 179: ## PART B -- #530 lcaisalane, closing pass 5, repair 5, 185 rows
 180: 
 181: ### S-B.1 -- ENUMERATION WRONG; pass 5 does not close.  Eight cells, verified from the handler.
 182: 
 183: `DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_isa)` makes exactly ONE 1-byte bus forward per access: the write arm forwards with `writeflag`
 184: (`dev_lca.c:299-302`), the read arm with `MEM_READ` then fills (`:305-311`).  The one-edit perturbations of a
 185: single-element (length, direction) sequence are DROP (0), SWAP (1, direction flipped), DUPLICATE (2, same direction),
 186: ADD-OTHER (2, mixed), over two arms = **8 cells**.  The contract names two (write ADD, read DUP).  Measured escapes:
 187: `WWtail`/`WW6` 185/185 (write DUP), `WWall` 169/185 and `RWall` 167/185 and `RD2post` 161/185 STUB-ONLY at lanes 0-2 --
 188: a direct RB1 breach.  `Wdrop`/`WXR`/`Rdrop`/`RXW` are all CAUGHT (136/140/127/143), so DROP and SWAP are not escapes,
 189: but they were never built before this pass and belong to the class.  **REPAIR 6.**
 190: 
 191: ### S-B.2 -- E1 UPHELD, and Q1 answered: the class is 8 cells, SWAP included, and it is bounded.
 192: 
 193: The class grows to all eight (E1 correct).  **Q1: SWAP is NOT a different class.**  It is a one-TOKEN edit
 194: (`MEM_READ` <-> `writeflag`) of the SAME single forward -- length stays 1, direction flips -- so it is a one-edit
 195: perturbation of the handler's own output and belongs to the forward-SHAPE class (the "forward-count" name was too
 196: narrow; the seat corrected its own six-cell derivation to eight for exactly this reason).  **The bound that keeps this
 197: from becoming unbounded is "one-edit perturbations of the SINGLE forward the handler emits,"** which is finite (8) and
 198: diff-closed.  It EXCLUDES the address-decode class (`:95-96`, lane/mask mutants) and any value-corruption class -- those
 199: are separate classes with their own rows, and repair 6 must not absorb them.  A closure that stopped at three of eight
 200: leaves the same shape open for a seventh pass; the amendment exists to end exactly that.
 201: 
 202: ### S-B.3 -- E3 UPHELD (the flagged disclosure is correct), with the reason stated so it is not misused.
 203: 
 204: deepseek's mutant is `if (data[ofs] != 0x42) return cpu->memory_rw(...)` -- the write-arm ADD-OTHER (fallthrough)
 205: SHAPE, additionally gated on the exact value 0x42.  The SHAPE is a floor cell (rowed via `Wpure`/the pit `PS` rows at
 206: 0x00 and 0x5a).  The EXACT-VALUE gating is genuinely clause-(e) debt: the handler tests **no bit** of the forwarded
 207: byte (`byte = data[ofs]`), so no value is a body-tested partition, and no finite row set (0x00/0x5a) covers 0x42.
 208: **No new row is owed; disclosure by class is correct** -- and the header now makes it
 209: (`lca_isalane_probe.py:133`, "a fallthrough keyed on an EXACT value is disclosed by class").  **E3 UPHELD.**
 210: 
 211: **Two conditions on that upholding, because E3 is correct only inside them:**
 212: 1. deepseek's finding is what FORCED the disclosure -- the contract previously claimed "gated on the data" closure
 213:    WITHOUT the exact-value exclusion (the L4 the seat flagged).  E3's phrasing "the class the contract ALREADY
 214:    discloses" understates that; the honest record is "deepseek caught the overclaim; it is now disclosed."  The
 215:    in-place correction (`:129-133`) discharges it.
 216: 2. **Repair 6 must NOT use E3 to skip rowing the three MEASURED joints** `cdxZL3`, `dNZtail`, `RD2z` (all 185/185).
 217:    Those are SHAPE floor cells (write-ADD at lane 3 x zero; write-ADD at tail x nonzero; read-DUP x zero-delivered) and
 218:    the seat's plan ROWS them cheaply on the new channels (stub3 zero store; `pitT ps5a t*`; `pl00_*`/`dlv`).  Only the
 219:    PURE exact-value keying (0x42, beyond {zero,nonzero}) is disclosed.  Do not let the exact-value disclosure swallow
 220:    the shape floor.
 221: 
 222: ### S-B.4 -- E2 UPHELD and DISCHARGED; Q2 answered.  My prior RB1 channels overturned.
 223: 
 224: E2 said "pending the seat's measurement, I am not adopting a channel I have not seen vary."  It has now varied:
 225: `noff` (flip-flops frozen) 77/150 with `live1` red first in all four sessions; base 150/150.  The 8253 counter
 226: flip-flops at 0x40/0x41/0x42 toggle on every counter access (`dev_8253.c:280-285`), separate per direction, are
 227: ARCHITECTURAL (cited source, `#440`), and CANNOT go silently vacuous -- unlike the stub, whose `nox` "implemented"
 228: control leaves every stub row GREEN and reds only the liveness rows.  **Q2:** move lanes 0-2 and the tails to the 8253;
 229: keep stub3 + its mandatory liveness row for lane 3 ONLY, because port 0x43 is the mode register and does not advance
 230: any flip-flop (`cdxZL3`, whose whole edit is at lane 3, is 150/150 on the 8253 -- structurally blind, measured).  So an
 231: emulator-TODO channel is acceptable at lane 3 precisely because no architectural alternator exists there; it is NOT
 232: acceptable as the SOLE carrier at lanes 0-2, where one does.  My prior RB1 named CMOS REG C and IIR for lanes 1-2;
 233: both are one-shot latches and are OVERTURNED.
 234: 
 235: **Constructional requirements repair 6 must carry (measured or read by the seat):** every phase opens with a control
 236: word (`:320-321` rewinds the pair, or phases leak parity); keep the programmed counter LARGE (an MSB write to counter 0
 237: calls `timer_add` with `hz = 1193182/counter`, `:191-196` -- a small counter installs a fast host timer); and the
 238: `floodclass` note that a 16-bit MSB write to counters 1/2 emits an UNLATCHED `fatal()` (`:198`, `:200`), 36 per run on
 239: a guest-drivable path -- correctly filed SEPARATELY, not ridden along.
 240: 
